WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 217: Spain: Philip II, the Spanish Armada, and the Silver Galleons of Potosí

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.600
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.800
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:11.600
[Rebecca] Today we conclude our exploration of Spain at the height of its imperial majesty.

4
00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:19.520
[Rebecca] In the 16th century, Spanish galleons crossed oceans of silver, while a paper-obsessed monarch commanded global crusades.

5
00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:21.840
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of Philip I.

6
00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:28.000
[Rebecca] The treasure of Potosi and the Spanish Armada.

7
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:39.440
[George] Inheriting the Spanish Empire, the Low Countries, parts of Italy, and the vast American colonies from his father, Emperor Charles V, in 1556, King Philip I.

8
00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:42.160
[George] was the most powerful monarch in Christendom.

9
00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:53.120
[George] Unlike his globetrotting father who fought in the saddle, Philip was an intensely reclusive, pious bureaucrat who governed an empire spanning four continents from a plain wooden desk.

10
00:00:53.120 --> 00:01:09.200
[George] In the foothills of the Sierra de Guadarama, thirty miles northwest of Madrid, Philip constructed El Escorial, a colossal, austere granite palace, monastery, and royal pantheon built in the shape of an overturned gridiron, commemorating the martyrdom of St.

11
00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:10.160
[George] Lawrence.

12
00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:23.280
[George] Working 14 hours a day, surrounded by thousands of dispatches from Peru, Mexico, Manila, and Flanders, Philip insisted on reading and annotating every royal document in his spidery handwriting.

13
00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:25.920
[George] Earning the moniker the king of paper.

14
00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:39.760
[George] Devoutly convinced that he was an instrument of divine providence, Philip subordinate all statecraft to the defense and global propagation of the Roman Catholic Church, transforming Spain into the military sword of the counter-reformation.

15
00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:51.440
[George] Philip maintained an obsessive surveillance over his vast empire, annotating thousands of state papers in his meticulous handwriting by candlelight in his drafty stone study at El Escorial.

16
00:01:51.440 --> 00:02:03.640
[George] His deep piety and ascetic habits earned him the awe of his subjects, but his bureaucratic paralysis frequently left military commanders in Flanders and the New World without orders or funding for months on end.

17
00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:10.920
[George] The colossal military power of Philip's empire was fueled by an unprecedented torrent of precious metal from the New World.

18
00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:31.960
[George] In 1545, in the freezing Andean highlands of modern Bolivia, an indigenous miner discovered the Cerro Rico, the rich mountain of Potosi, an 80% pure vein of silver so vast it became the largest silver mine in human history, spawning a boomton higher than the clouds with a population exceeding London or Paris.

19
00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:47.320
[George] Using the Mercury amalgamation process, millions of ounces of refined silver ingots and stamped pieces of eight were packed on llama trains down to the Pacific, shipped across the Isthmus of Panama, and loaded onto heavily armed royal galleons.

20
00:02:47.320 --> 00:03:01.320
[George] To protect the silver from English and Dutch privateers like Francis Drake, Spain developed the Flota de Indias, the annual treasure fleet system, sailing in convoy twice a year under the escort of heavily gunned galleons.

21
00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:14.840
[George] These fleets carried billions in silver to the royal mints of Seville, financing Spanish armies across Europe, but paradoxically unleashing devastating hyperinflation that crippled native Spanish domestic manufacturing.

22
00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:27.720
[George] In the suffocating depths of the Cerro Rico at Potosi, tens of thousands of indigenous and African miners labored under the forced labor system of the Mita, mining veins of pure silver four miles above sea level.

23
00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:39.800
[George] The resulting silver ingots and pieces of eight were stamped at the royal mint and transported across the Isthmus of Panama by mule trains before loading onto the heavily armed galleons of the Carrera de Indias.

24
00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:43.320
[George] Before confronting Protestant Northern Europe, Philip I.

25
00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:48.080
[George] faced the menacing westward expansion of the Ottoman Empire in the Mediterranean.

26
00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:57.920
[George] In 1571, the Ottoman Turks conquered the Venetian island of Cyprus, brutally flaying its Venetian defender Mark Antonio Braggadin alive.

27
00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:11.360
[George] In response, Pope Pugh's V formed the Holy League, uniting Spain, Venice, and the Papal States under the supreme naval command of Philip's 24-year-old illegitimate half-brother, Don John of Austria.

28
00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:22.480
[George] On October 7, 1571, in the Gulf of Patras off western Greece, the two largest galley fleets in human history collided at the historic Battle of Lepanto.

29
00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:30.320
[George] Over 400 Ord War galleys, carrying 70,000 soldiers and rowers, clashed in furious hand-to-hand combat.

30
00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:46.640
[George] Supported by six massive Venetian galleys, floating gun batteries that shattered the Turkish battle line with heavy broadsides, the Holy League annihilated the Ottoman fleet, sinking or capturing 200 galleys and freeing 12,000 Christian galley slaves.

31
00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:54.880
[George] Among the wounded Spanish soldiers was Miguel de Cervantes, who lost the use of his left hand, later writing Don Quixote.

32
00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:59.200
[George] Lepanto broke the aura of Ottoman naval invincibility forever.

33
00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:12.640
[George] At the Gulf of Patras in October 1571, the Holy League's fleet under Don John of Austria smashed into the Ottoman galley line, engaging in brutal hand to hand combat across blood slick wooden decks.

34
00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:24.960
[George] The victory shattered the myth of Ottoman naval invincibility in the Mediterranean and sent celebratory church bells ringing across Catholic Europe, establishing Spain as the supreme crusader empire.

35
00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:32.200
[George] By the 1580s, Philip's attention turned toward his mortal rival, Protestant England under Queen Elizabeth I.

36
00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:47.880
[George] English sea dogs like Sir Francis Drake had plundered Spanish ports in the Caribbean and Cadiz, singing the King of Spain's beard, while Elizabeth sent English gold and troops to support Protestant Dutch rebels fighting the Spanish army of Flanders.

37
00:05:47.880 --> 00:06:02.360
[George] Following the execution of the Catholic Mary, Queen of Scots, in 1587, Philip resolved upon the great enterprise of England, an invasion to overthrow Elizabeth, restore Catholicism, and end English piracy.

38
00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:12.520
[George] Mobilising the resources of his global empire in the port of Lisbon, Philip assembled the Grand Y Felicissima Armada, the great and most fortunate navy.

39
00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:21.480
[George] Commanded by the Duke of Medina Sidonia, a wealthy grandee who confessed he had never fought a naval battle and suffered violently from seasickness.

40
00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:28.680
[George] The armada comprised 130 ships, including twenty colossal Portuguese and Castilian galleons.

41
00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:36.280
[George] 8,000 sailors and 19,000 soldiers, carrying 300 priests and holy banners blessed by the Pope.

42
00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:47.880
[George] The plan was to sail up the English Channel, link up with 30,000 veteran Spanish infantry under the Duke of Parma in the Netherlands, and ferry them across the Thames estuary to conquer London.

43
00:06:47.880 --> 00:07:02.120
[George] The logistical undertaking of the Enterprise of England was staggering: 130 ships, including twenty massive Portuguese and Castilian galleons, carrying over 8,000 sailors and 19,000 hardened soldiers.

44
00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:14.280
[George] Philip envisioned a seamless junction off the Flemish coast with the Duke of Parma's veteran infantry, who would be ferried across the English Channel in flat-bottomed barges to march directly upon Protestant London.

45
00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:24.240
[George] In late July 1588, the crescent formation of the Spanish Armada was sighted off the coast of Plymouth, stretching for seven miles across the horizon.

46
00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:28.960
[George] English beacon fires blazed across the hilltops, rallying the nation.

47
00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:40.240
[George] The English fleet, commanded by Lord Howard of Effingham and Sir Francis Drake, possessed smaller, faster, low-slung race-built galleons equipped with long-range culveran cannon.

48
00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:45.040
[George] For a week, the two fleets fought a running artillery duel up the English Channel.

49
00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:54.240
[George] The tight Spanish crescent formation held firm, but the English refused to close within range for the traditional Spanish tactic of grappling and boarding.

50
00:07:54.240 --> 00:08:01.280
[George] On August 6, the Armada anchored off Calais in exposed French waters, awaiting news of Parma's army.

51
00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:06.800
[George] But Parma's flat-bottomed invasion barges were blockaded in port by Dutch flyboats.

52
00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:21.040
[George] At midnight on August 7, Drake launched eight hellburners, abandoned wooden ships packed with pitch, tar and gunpowder, set ablaze with sails fully set, drifting on the tide straight into the crowded Spanish anchorage.

53
00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:30.240
[George] Terrified of floating explosions, Spanish captains panicked, cut their heavy anchor cables in the pitch black, and scattered into the stormy sea.

54
00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:42.560
[George] As the armada advanced up the English Channel in a tight, impenetrable crescent formation, English commanders Lord Howard and Sir Francis Drake harried the flanks with long range culveran artillery fire.

55
00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:57.200
[George] When the Spanish anchored in the exposed roadstead off Calais, Drake launched eight tar-soaked, burning fire ships into the dark harbour, panicking the Spanish captains into cutting their anchor cables and scattering their fleet into the stormy North Sea.

56
00:08:57.200 --> 00:09:08.040
[George] At dawn on August 8, with the Spanish fleet disorganized and drifting without anchors, the English fleet attacked at close quarters off the Flemish coast at the Battle of Gravellines.

57
00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:19.560
[George] Closing to point blank range, English gun crews, trained to reload five times faster than the Spanish, hammered the timber hulls of the Spanish galleons with relentless broadsides.

58
00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:27.480
[George] Blood flowed from the scuppers, five major galleons were sunk or run aground, and hundreds of Spanish soldiers were killed.

59
00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:39.800
[George] Medina Sidonia fought with personal bravery, but running low on cannon shot and water, and blocked from retreating south by the English fleet and shifting southwest winds, he faced disaster.

60
00:09:39.800 --> 00:09:53.080
[George] The Armada had no choice but to escape north into the treacherous North Sea, embarking on an agonizing, perilous voyage around the wild, storm-lashed coasts of northern Scotland and western Ireland to reach Spain.

61
00:09:53.080 --> 00:10:05.000
[George] Queen Elizabeth addressed her troops at Tilbury with immortal words I know I have the body but of a weak and feeble woman, but I have the heart and stomach of a king, and of a king of England too.

62
00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:18.680
[George] The following morning at the Battle of Gravellines, English galleons closed within point blank range, battering the disorganized Spanish ships with heavy cannon broadsides until the galleons took on water and fouled their rigging.

63
00:10:18.680 --> 00:10:35.160
[George] As ammunition ran out and the wind shifted violently northward, Medina Sidonia realized that invasion was impossible, and the prevailing gales, celebrated in England as the Protestant wind, forced the battered Armada into the treacherous northern waters around Scotland.

64
00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:48.720
[George] In September 1588, unseasonably violent Atlantic tempests, celebrated in England as the Protestant wind, battered the battered Spanish Armada as it rounded the rugged cliffs of the Hebrides and western Ireland.

65
00:10:48.880 --> 00:11:01.840
[George] Lacking anchors, their rigging shredded, their crews dying of typhus, gangrene and thirst, dozens of majestic galleons were smashed against the jagged rocks of Sligo, Galway, and County Kerry.

66
00:11:01.840 --> 00:11:09.120
[George] Over 5,000 Spanish soldiers and sailors drowned in the surf or were slaughtered by English garrisons on the beaches.

67
00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:16.160
[George] Only sixty-seven broken vessels and ten thousand half-starved survivors staggered back into the ports of northern Spain.

68
00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:28.480
[George] When Medina Sidonia arrived at El Escorial to confess the disaster, Philip I showed extraordinary stoic dignity, remarking gently, I sent you to fight with men, and not with the weather.

69
00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:30.320
[George] Let God's will be done.

70
00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:32.880
[George] Commemorative English medals proclaimed.

71
00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:38.000
[George] Flavit Jehovah et Disapati's sunt, God blue, and they were scattered.

72
00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:49.360
[George] Though Spain remained a formidable superpower for decades, the defeat of the Armada marked the beginning of the slow eclipse of the Spanish Golden Age and the rise of British maritime supremacy.

73
00:11:49.360 --> 00:12:03.280
[George] Along the rocky, fog-shrouded coasts of the Hebrides and western Ireland, violent Atlantic gales shattered dozens of crippled galleons on the reefs, leaving thousands of starving Spanish sailors to drown or be slaughtered on the beaches.

74
00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:18.240
[George] The defeat of the Armada did not immediately destroy the Spanish Empire, but it exposed the limits of Philip's imperial overreach, leaving behind enormous royal debts and signalling the gradual rise of English and Dutch maritime supremacy.

75
00:12:18.800 --> 00:12:26.240
[Rebecca] The Spanish Armada demonstrated that even the mightiest empires must bow before the tempests of nature and history.

76
00:12:26.240 --> 00:12:32.920
[Rebecca] Across the Rhine, the heart of Europe was about to be torn apart by an intellectual and spiritual earthquake.

77
00:12:33.160 --> 00:12:51.320
[Rebecca] Next week, we begin our exploration of Germany with episode 218, Martin Luther, the 95 Theses at Wittenberg, and the Protestant Reformation, the Lightning Bolt that Made a Monk, the Castle Church door, the Diet of Worms, and the translation of the Bible that unified the German language.

78
00:12:51.320 --> 00:12:56.120
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

